  "ce822b6b4b5601ebfe2ec0b80c7fba40277a8208148ac934a15ff064f7eb6a53": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Meningioma was considered because the lesion involved the sphenoid wing and presented as a dural-based enhancing mass — “Intraosseous sphenoid wing meningioma can closely mimic FD both clinically and radiologically.”\n2. Metastatic carcinoma was considered given the bony destruction and mass effect, but it was excluded because of the absence of epithelial tumor cells — “The metastatic carcinoma is another close differential at this site. However, the epithelial nature of neoplastic cells encountered in a metastatic carcinoma is the distinguishing factor.”\n3. Malignant transformation of fibrous dysplasia was considered in the setting of cortical breach and soft-tissue extension but was deemed unlikely prior to histology — “Cortical destruction with soft tissue extension is usually indicative of malignant transformation and thus needs a thorough histological assessment.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- The long-standing left fronto-orbital swelling and expansive lytic bony lesion involving the superior and lateral walls of the orbit are characteristic of Fibrous Dysplasia.\n- The patient's symptoms, including progressive deterioration of vision, and imaging findings, such as a heterogeneous enhancing mass on MRI and an expansive lytic bony lesion on CT, are consistent with Fibrous Dysplasia.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: \"Meningioma was considered because the lesion involved the sphenoid wing and presented as a dural-based enhancing mass — “Intraosseous sphenoid wing meningioma can closely mimic FD both clinically and radiologically.”\"\n- Potential match 1: \"The long-standing left fronto-orbital swelling and expansive lytic bony lesion involving the superior and lateral walls of the orbit are characteristic of Fibrous Dysplasia.\"\n    - Pros: Both mention a lesion and its location (sphenoid wing/fronto-orbital). Both discuss imaging characteri