  "89adb5758049395fc6019b9206f6d970b1cd3c118aa4e45f8dabb5da76af11dd": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Sepsis was considered given DIC, shock, lactic acidosis, and multiorgan dysfunction, but “All peripheral blood cultures and urine cultures were negative.”\n2. Serotonin syndrome was contemplated due to hyperthermia and autonomic instability, but “With negative urine screening for several common SRIs and other drugs with serotonergic activity, serotonin syndrome is less likely in our patient.”\n3. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome was unlikely because “NMS … has a slow onset and progression (i.e., days) . Our patient had a rapid progression and negative drug screening for many typical and atypical neuroleptics.”\n4. Malignant hyperthermia was excluded since “MH presents within minutes to hours of exposure to volatile anesthetics and/or depolarizing muscle relaxants … he had hyperthermia, hyperkalemia, and elevated creatine kinase prior to the administration of succinylcholine for intubation.”\n5. Drug-induced fever was unlikely as it “occurs 7–10 days after starting a medication and is often limited to fever.”\n6. Oxidative phosphorylation uncoupling from salicylates was excluded by “negative drug screening results for salicylates.”\n7. Anticholinergic toxicity was ruled out because “our patient’s drug screen was negative for several such agents.”\n8. Primary arrhythmia from toxin was deemed improbable since the “wide complex tachycardia seen in our patient was likely due to hyperkalemia.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient has severe rhabdomyolysis on laboratory studies, elevated temperature of 107°F, hyperkalemia, hyperuricemia, decline in urine output over 24-hour periods, and need for daily hemodialysis for volume control and solute clearance, which are all consistent with rhabdomyolysis.\n2. The patient has severe rhabdomyolysis on laboratory studies, elevated temperature and agitation, which are common symptoms of rhabdomyolysis, and other symptoms such as dark urine and elevated creatine kinase that are consistent with the diagnosis.\n3. The patient has agitation and erratic behavior, ingestion and insufflation of bath salts, and elevated pulse of 93/min, which are all consistent with sympathomimetic toxicity.\n4. The patient has agitation and erratic behavior, which are consistent with sympathomimetic toxicity, and a history of ingestion and insufflation of 'bath salts', which are known to cause sympathomimetic toxicity, as well as other symptoms such as hyperthermia and hypertension.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Sepsis was considered given DIC, shock, lactic acidosis, and multiorgan dysfunction, but “All peripheral blood cultures and urine cultures were negative.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses sepsis or negative cultures.\n  - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Serotonin syndrome was contemplated due to hyperthermia and autonomic instability, but “With negative urine screening for several common SRIs and other drugs with serotonergic activity, serotonin syndrome is less likely in our patient.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses serotonin syndrome or negative drug screens for SRIs.\n  - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Neuroleptic malignant syndrome was unlikely because “NMS … has a slow onset and progression (i.e., days) . Our patient had a rapid progression and negative drug screening for many typical and atypical neuroleptics.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome, its onset, or negative drug screens for neuroleptics.\n  - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Malignant hyperthermia was excluded since “MH presents within minutes to hours of exposure to volatile anesthetics and/or depolarizing muscle relaxants … he had hyperthermia, hyperkalemia, and elevated creatine kinase prior to the administration of succinylcholine for intubation.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses Malignant Hyperthermia or its association with anesthetics/muscle relaxants. While predicted statements mention hyperthermia, hyperkalemia, and elevated creatine kinase, they attribute these to rhabdomyolysis or sympathomimetic toxicity, not as reasons to exclude MH.\n  - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Drug-induced fever was unlikely as it “occurs 7–10 days after starting a medication and is often limited to fever.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses drug-induced fever or its typical onset.\n  - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Oxidative phosphorylation uncoupling from salicylates was excluded by “negative drug screening results for salicylates.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses salicylates or negative drug screens for them.\n  - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 7: Anticholinergic toxicity was ruled out because “our patient’s drug screen was negative for several such agents.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses anticholinergic toxicity or negative drug screens for anticholinergic agents.\n  - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 8: Primary arrhythmia from toxin was deemed improbable since the “wide complex tachycardia seen in our patient was likely due to hyperkalemia.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None\n  - Pros: No predicted statement discusses primary arrhythmia from toxin or attributes wide complex tachycardia to hyperkalemia. While hyperkalemia is mentioned in predicted statement 1, it's in the context of rhabdomyolysis, not as an explanation for arrhythmia.\n  - Cons: