nt with ICI-induced pneumonitis,\" directly supporting the suspicion of the diagnosis.\n    - Cons: The groundtruth mentions \"timing\" which is not explicitly in the predicted reason, but the predicted reason focuses on the clinical findings themselves.\n- Potential match 2: Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2: \"The patient has new bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, elevated C-reactive protein and surfactant protein D levels, negative sputum cultures and procalcitonin, and no improvement with antibiotics, which are all consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. Additionally, the patient has fever and dyspnea, which are also common symptoms of this condition.\"\n    - Pros: Similar to Predicted Reason 1, this statement also lists multiple findings (imaging, biomarkers, symptoms) and explicitly states they are \"consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis.\" It also includes the exclusion of infection and lack of antibiotic response, which strengthens the case for ICI-induced pneumonitis.\n    - Cons: Similar to Predicted Reason 1, \"timing\" is not explicitly mentioned.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 8: Steroid refractoriness was established by persistent infiltrates and worsened oxygen requirement despite two high‐dose steroid courses — “radiographic findings taken after these treatments showed a persistent infiltrative shadow, and the patient required increased oxygen support, with the flow rate rising from 2 to 4 L/min,” and “no significant changes in the respiratory status of the patient.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons focus on the initial presentation and ruling out other conditions, not the response to steroid treatment.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: No match.\n- Groundtruth 2: No match.\n- Groundtruth 3: Matches Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2 because it mentions \"no improvement with antibiotics.\"\n- Groundtruth 4: No match.\n- Groundtruth 5: No match.\n- Groundtruth 6: Matches Predicted 