  "cd76afb3d210f0294679c64257d0e28de581fa6825764575e5595443f560f91a": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Primary adrenal lymphoma was excluded because it would be FDG-avid; “both are hypermetabolic and FDG avid… absence of FDG uptake.”\n2. Secondary adrenal involvement by lymphoma was excluded as metastases require FDG PET hypermetabolism; “any secondary localization of the disease… have to show FDG PET hypermetabolism.”\n3. Cushing’s syndrome was excluded because neither ACTH-dependent nor independent hyperplasia is spontaneously reversible and the patient had no hypercortisolism signs; “neither is spontaneously reversible… our patient had no signs and/or symptoms of hypercortisolism.”\n4. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and pheochromocytoma were excluded due to absence of characteristic clinical features and lack of spontaneous regression; “excluded because of the absence of clinical symptoms suspected for pheocromocytoma and because these forms do not show spontaneous regression.”\n5. Adrenal hemorrhage was excluded as CT densitometry did not show hyperattenuation and follow-up imaging lacked cystic or calcific involution; “CT densitometry of the glands did not show the hyperattenuation… typical for haemorrhage… showed neither cystic nor calcific involution.”\n6. Adrenal cystic lesions were excluded because CT densitometry was not compatible with fluid; “cystic lesions… can be excluded due to the CT densitometry not compatible with fluid lesions.”\n7. Infectious adrenalitis was excluded given absence of clinical signs and lack of calcified relics; “no suggestive clinical data for adrenalitis… infection often leaves calcified relics… which we did not found.”\n8. Adrenocortical pharmacotoxicity was excluded as there was no history of drugs known to cause adrenal toxicity; “medical history did not reveal compounds capable of causing adrenal toxicity.”\n9. Stress-related adrenal hyperplasia was considered most likely due to lymphomatous proliferation activating the HPA axis; “suggesting the activation of the HPA axis by hormone-like bioactive factors secreted by lymphomatous / tumor cells as the cause.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Testicular cancer\nEvidence: The patient presents with painless right testicular swelling, enlargement and inhomogeneity of the right testis and epididymis on ultrasound, and intense uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, all of which are consistent with testicular cancer. Additionally, the testicular ultrasound findings and intense uptake on PET/CT are key diagnostic features for this condition.\nDiagnosis: Adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology\nEvidence: The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology. The normal electrolytes and blood pressure also support this diagnosis, as adrenal adenomas are often non-functioning.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Primary adrenal lymphoma was excluded because it would be FDG-avid; “both are hypermetabolic and FDG avid… absence of FDG uptake.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    - Pros: This statement explicitly mentions \"absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT,\" which directly aligns with the reason for excluding primary adrenal lymphoma (which would be FDG-avid).\n    - Cons: The predicted statement is used to support adrenal adenoma, not explicitly to exclude lymphoma. However, the core piece of information (absence of FDG uptake) is present and used in a diagnostic context.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Secondary adrenal involvement by lymphoma was excluded as metastases require FDG PET hypermetabolism; “any secondary localization of the disease… have to show FDG PET hypermetabolism.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    - Pros: Similar to GR1, this predicted statement mentions \"absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT.\" This directly contradicts the requirement for FDG PET hypermetabolism in secondary adrenal involvement by lymphoma, thus serving as an exclusionary criterion.\n    - Cons: The predicted statement is used to support adrenal adenoma, not explicitly to exclude lymphoma. However, the core piece of information (absence of FDG uptake) is present and used in a diagnostic context.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Cushing’s syndrome was excluded because neither ACTH-dependent nor independent hyperplasia is spontaneously reversible and the patient had no hypercortisolism signs; “neither is spontaneously reversible… our patient had no signs and/or symptoms of hypercortisolism.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons focus on imaging findings (CT, PET/CT) and basic lab values (electrolytes, blood pressure), but do not mention hypercortisolism signs or spontaneous reversibility.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and pheochromocytoma were excluded due to absence of characteristic clinical features and lack of spontaneous regression; “excluded because of the absence of clinical symptoms suspected for pheocromocytoma and because these forms do not show spontaneous regression.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not discuss specific clinical features for these conditions or spontaneous regression.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Adrenal hemorrhage was excluded as CT densitometry did not show hyperattenuation and follow-up imaging lacked cystic or calcific involution; “CT densitometry of the glands did not show the hyperattenuation… typical for haemorrhage… showed neither cystic nor calcific involution.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    - Pros: This statement mentions \"homogeneous attenuation on CT.\" While not explicitly stating \"did not show hyperattenuation,\" homogeneous attenuation implies the absence of the hyperattenuation typical for hemorrhage.\n    - Cons: It doesn't mention the lack of cystic or calcific involution. The match is partial.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Adrenal cystic lesions were excluded because CT densitometry was not compatible with fluid; “cystic lesions… can be excluded due to the CT densitometry not compatible with fluid lesions.”\n- Potential match from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    - Pros: This statement mentions \"homogeneous attenuation on CT.\" Homogeneous attenuation is generally not compatible with fluid-filled cystic lesions, which would typically show very low attenuation.\n    - Cons: It doesn't explicitly state \"not compatible with fluid.\" The connection is inferential.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 7: Infectious adrenalitis was excluded given absence of clinical signs and lack of calcified relics; “no suggestive clinical data for adrenalitis… infection often leaves calcified relics… which we did not found.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not discuss clinical signs of adrenalitis or calcified relics.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 8: Adrenocortical pharmacotoxicity was excluded as there was no history of drugs known to cause adrenal toxicity; “medical history did not reveal compounds capable of causing adrenal toxicity.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not discuss medical history or drug use.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 9: Stress-related adrenal hyperplasia was considered most likely due to lymphomatous proliferation activating the HPA axis; “suggesting the activation of the HPA axis by hormone-like bioactive factors secreted by lymphomatous / tumor cells as the cause.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not discuss stress-related hyperplasia or HPA axis activation.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- GR1: Matches with \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\" due to the mention of \"absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT.\"\n- GR2: Matches with \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\" due to the mention of \"absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT.\"\n- GR3: No match.\n- GR4: No match.\n- GR5: Matches with \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\" due to \"homogeneous attenuation on CT\" implying absence of hyperattenuation.\n- GR6: Matches with \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\" due to \"homogeneous attenuation on CT\" implying not compatible with fluid.\n- GR7: No match.\n- GR8: No match.\n- GR9: No match.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [\n      \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    ],\n    \"6\": [\n      \"The patient has new bilateral macronodular adrenal enlargement with homogeneous attenuation on CT and absence of uptake on 18 FDG PET/CT, which are characteristic of adrenal adenoma or other adrenal pathology.\"\n    ],\n    \"7\": [],\n    \"8\": [],\n    \"9\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
