rders — “Histopathologically, some of the described lesions present themselves primarily as granulomatous chronic inflammation, such as…syphilitic osteomyelitis” — but excluded by negative Treponema pallidum serology.\nNo direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n\nGroundtruth 5: Granulomatosis with polyangiitis — considered among granulomatous mastoid lesions — “Histopathologically, some of the described lesions present themselves primarily as granulomatous chronic inflammation, such as…granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis)” — but excluded by negative ANCA testing.\nNo direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n\nGroundtruth 6: Sarcoidosis — considered as a granulomatous cause of bone lesions — “Histopathologically, some of the described lesions present themselves primarily as granulomatous chronic inflammation, such as…sarcoidosis” — but excluded by normal serum ACE and lack of pulmonary lymphadenopathy.\nNo direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n\nGroundtruth 7: Cholesteatoma — considered given chronic ear discharge and canal polyps — “Accumulation of exfoliated keratin produced from stratified squamous epithelium” — but excluded by imaging that showed diffuse osteolysis rather than localized epitympanic debris.\nPredicted 3: The patient presents with progressive unsteadiness, recurrent falls, bilateral osteolysis of the mastoids, and profound right-sided hearing loss, which are all consistent with cholesteatoma. The presence of polypoid tissue and purulent secretion in the right external canal further supports this diagnosis.\nPros: Both statements identify Cholesteatoma as a diagnosis. The groundtruth mentions \"chronic ear discharge and canal polyps,\" and the predicted statement mentions \"polypoid tissue and purulent secretion in the right external canal,\" which are very similar findings. The predicted statement also includes \"bilateral osteolysis of the mastoids,\" which is a relevant finding for cholesteatoma.\nCons