  "ee842a05b7c7f5699da54316058fc7842cf23387aec99df66067c9970c64012f": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Primary colorectal adenocarcinoma — “primary colorectal tumours tend to demonstrate the opposite pattern being positive for CDX2 and negative for TTF1…”\n2. Primary gastrointestinal neuroendocrine tumour — “Although neuroendocrine tumours can arise in any body region 95% of all small cell carcinomas are lung origin…”\n3. Metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma — “Metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma is considered less likely as there is no cytoplasmic CK20 staining.”\n4. Metastatic small cell carcinoma of lung — “positive immunostaining for TTF1 with negative staining for Caudal-type Homeobox 2 (CDX2) lead us to suspect a lung origin tumour…”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient presented with acute right lower-quadrant abdominal pain, peritonism, and a perforated appendix on CT scan, which are all consistent with perforated appendicitis.\n2. The patient's febrile and hypotensive state also support this diagnosis.\n3. The patient developed fever, hypotension, and tachycardia after surgery, and had four-quadrant purulent contamination on laparotomy, which are all indicative of postoperative sepsis.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Statement 1: \"Primary colorectal adenocarcinoma — “primary colorectal tumours tend to demonstrate the opposite pattern being positive for CDX2 and negative for TTF1…”\"\n- This statement discusses a specific type of cancer (colorectal adenocarcinoma) and its immunohistochemical staining pattern (positive for CDX2, negative for TTF1).\n- Potential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None. The predicted reasons discuss perforated appendicitis and postoperative sepsis, which are completely different diagnoses and do not involve immunohistochemistry or cancer types.\n- Pros for no match: The predicted reasons are about acute inflammatory/infectious conditions, not cancer pathology.\n- Cons for no match: None.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Statement 2: \"Primary gastrointestinal neuroendocrine tumour — “Although neuroendocrine tumours can arise in any body region 95% of all small cell carcinomas are lung origin…”\"\n- This statement discusses neuroendocrine tumors, their origin, and the prevalence of small cell carcinomas in the lung.\n- Potential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None. The predicted reasons are about appendicitis and sepsis, not neuroendocrine tumors or their origins.\n- Pros for no match: The predicted reasons are unrelated to tumor types or origins.\n- Cons for no match: None.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Statement 3: \"Metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma — “Metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma is considered less likely as there is no cytoplasmic CK20 staining.”\"\n- This statement discusses a specific type of metastatic cancer (Merkel cell carcinoma) and a reason for its unlikelihood based on the absence of cytoplasmic CK20 staining.\n- Potential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None. The predicted reasons are about appendicitis and sepsis, not Merkel cell carcinoma or CK20 staining.\n- Pros for no match: The predicted reasons are unrelated to specific cancer types or immunohistochemical markers.\n- Cons for no match: None.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Statement 4: \"Metastatic small cell carcinoma of lung — “positive immunostaining for TTF1 with negative staining for Caudal-type Homeobox 2 (CDX2) lead us to suspect a lung origin tumour…”\"\n- This statement discusses metastatic small cell carcinoma of the lung and the immunohistochemical findings (positive TTF1, negative CDX2) that support a lung origin.\n- Potential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons: None. The predicted reasons are about appendicitis and sepsis, not small cell carcinoma, lung origin, or immunohistochemistry.\n- Pros for no match: The predicted reasons are unrelated to cancer diagnosis or specific staining patterns.\n- Cons for no match: None.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\nThere are no matching statements between the Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons and the Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The groundtruth reasons are focused on differential diagnoses of various cancers based on immunohistochemistry, while the predicted reasons are focused on acute inflammatory/infectious conditions (perforated appendicitis and postoperative sepsis) based on clinical presentation, imaging, and surgical findings.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
