    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Cranial nerve palsy — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\n2. Schwannoma — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\n3. Thyroid eye disease — “The globes were found to be unremarkable with no measured proptosis.”\n4. Orbital mass — “Noted is a right orbital linear soft tissue lesion … with enhancement pattern and signal intensity characteristics following extraocular muscle on all sequences.”\n5. Intracranial mass lesion — “There were no suprasellar or parasellar mass lesions.”\n6. Congenital syndromes (Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, CFEOM) — “Clinical manifestation of an accessory extraocular muscle frequently occurs in childhood, along with numerous other congenital causes, such as Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, and CFEOM.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- The patient has a history of an anomalous band in the right orbit, elevation deficit of the right eye, and a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, all of which are consistent with Adhesion Syndrome.\n- The patient has a long-standing history of diplopia and has been found to be orthotropic in primary position but with an elevation deficit of the right eye, which are all consistent with strabismus. The presence of vertical misalignment also supports this diagnosis.\n- The patient presents with persistent vertical misalignment, diplopia that remained stable in primary gaze, and an elevation deficit of the right eye, which are all characteristic of Strabismus.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Cranial nerve palsy — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons focus on supporting Adhesion Syndrome and Strabismus, not ruling out other conditions like cranial nerve palsy.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Schwannoma — “This scan would also rule out other possible causes such as nerve palsy and schwannoma.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. Similar to cranial nerve palsy, the predicted reasons do not discuss ruling out schwannoma.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Thyroid eye disease — “The globes were found to be unremarkable with no measured proptosis.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not mention thyroid eye disease or proptosis.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Orbital mass — “Noted is a right orbital linear soft tissue lesion … with enhancement pattern and signal intensity characteristics following extraocular muscle on all sequences.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient has a history of an anomalous band in the right orbit, elevation deficit of the right eye, and a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, all of which are consistent with Adhesion Syndrome.\"\n    - Pros: This statement explicitly mentions \"a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion,\" which directly corresponds to the \"right orbital linear soft tissue lesion\" in the ground truth. It also places it in the orbit.\n    - Cons: The ground truth statement provides more detail about the lesion's characteristics (enhancement pattern, signal intensity, following extraocular muscle), which is not fully captured in the predicted statement. However, the core finding of a \"right orbital linear soft tissue lesion\" is present.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Intracranial mass lesion — “There were no suprasellar or parasellar mass lesions.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not discuss intracranial mass lesions.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Congenital syndromes (Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, CFEOM) — “Clinical manifestation of an accessory extraocular muscle frequently occurs in childhood, along with numerous other congenital causes, such as Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, and CFEOM.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not mention these specific congenital syndromes. While \"anomalous band\" might be considered congenital, it's not directly linked to these specific syndromes in the predicted text.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: No match.\n- Groundtruth 2: No match.\n- Groundtruth 3: No match.\n- Groundtruth 4: Matches \"The patient has a history of an anomalous band in the right orbit, elevation deficit of the right eye, and a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, all of which are consistent with Adhesion Syndrome.\"\n- Groundtruth 5: No match.\n- Groundtruth 6: No match.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of an anomalous band in the right orbit, elevation deficit of the right eye, and a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, all of which are consistent with Adhesion Syndrome.\"\n    ],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n</diagnostic_comparison>",
