  "1089a275097d524f971949d194708d76c3d1a3d2e97a688bd2a6ced4414fa60d": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Lymphoma — “Another case of LELC was originally misdiagnosed as lymphoma or pseudolymphoma as a result of a dense lymphocytic component concealing the elusive neoplastic cells; ... immunohistochemical markers with cytokeratin and leukocyte common antigen, to differentiate between lymphomas and lymphoepithelioma-like carcinomas.”\n2. Medullary carcinoma — “LELC of the breast and medullary carcinomas share the presence of syncytial growth patterns, a dense lymphocytic infiltrate, and the absence of estrogen receptors”; “medullary carcinoma being well circumscribed and demarcated as compared to LELC of the breast, ... syncytial growth patterns covering an area greater than 75%”; “medullary carcinomas are usually E-cadherin positive, while LELC of the breast are negative.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient has a palpable breast mass, a spiculated lesion on breast MRI, and architectural distortion on MRI, which are all consistent with invasive ductal carcinoma.\n2. The biopsy also shows cohesive nests of neoplastic epithelial cells, further supporting this diagnosis.\n3. The epithelial cells are negative for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2/neu, which is consistent with triple-negative breast cancer.\n4. However, the clinical course is not described as aggressive, which is a common feature of this disease.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1 (Lymphoma):\n- \"Lymphoma — “Another case of LELC was originally misdiagnosed as lymphoma or pseudolymphoma as a result of a dense lymphocytic component concealing the elusive neoplastic cells; ... immunohistochemical markers with cytokeratin and leukocyte common antigen, to differentiate between lymphomas and lymphoepithelioma-like carcinomas.”\"\n\nPotential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- None of the predicted reasons discuss lymphoma, pseudolymphoma, lymphocytic components, or specific immunohistochemical markers like cytokeratin or leukocyte common antigen. The predicted reasons focus on invasive ductal carcinoma and triple-negative breast cancer based on imaging, biopsy findings, and receptor status.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2 (Medullary carcinoma):\n- \"Medullary carcinoma — “LELC of the breast and medullary carcinomas share the presence of syncytial growth patterns, a dense lymphocytic infiltrate, and the absence of estrogen receptors”; “medullary carcinoma being well circumscribed and demarcated as compared to LELC of the breast, ... syncytial growth patterns covering an area greater than 75%”; “medullary carcinomas are usually E-cadherin positive, while LELC of the breast are negative.”\"\n\nPotential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Predicted Reason 3: \"The epithelial cells are negative for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2/neu, which is consistent with triple-negative breast cancer.\"\n    - Pros: This statement explicitly mentions \"absence of estrogen receptors,\" which is a shared feature between LELC and medullary carcinoma as stated in the groundtruth. The groundtruth also mentions \"dense lymphocytic infiltrate\" and \"syncytial growth patterns\" as shared features, which are often seen in medullary carcinoma, and medullary carcinoma is frequently triple-negative.\n    - Cons: The predicted reason directly links the receptor negativity to \"triple-negative breast cancer\" rather than explicitly discussing medullary carcinoma as a differential or a type of breast cancer that shares these features. It doesn't mention syncytial growth patterns, dense lymphocytic infiltrate, or E-cadherin status.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- For Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1 (Lymphoma), there are no matching statements in the Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n- For Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2 (Medullary carcinoma), Predicted Diagnostic Reason 3 is a partial match because it mentions the \"absence of estrogen receptors,\" which is a characteristic shared by medullary carcinoma and LELC as per the groundtruth.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The epithelial cells are negative for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2/neu, which is consistent with triple-negative breast cancer.\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n</diagnostic_comparison>",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
  "edba59214a3653c562e53e41eb121b4087fa3200df848ff41d1c6ed1a54865ba": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Mesenchymal submucosal tumors (including GIST, leiomyoma, fibroma, and malignant variants) were considered but the lesion’s characteristic fat attenuation on CT favored a lipoma — “abdominal CT scan displayed a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric formation with fat density measuring 6*5 cm in the antropyloric region of the stomach.”\n2. Primary gastric malignancy was considered but endoscopic biopsy revealed no malignant features — “biopsy results indicated the absence of malignant signs.”\n3. Although exceptionally rare synchronous gastric carcinomas have been described, there was no evidence of a coincidental carcinoma in this case — “exceptionally rare cases of synchronous gastric carcinomas have been described, thought to be coincidental.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Diagnosis: Gastric Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumor (GIST)\n- Evidence: The patient presents with a submucosal lesion in the gastric antrum with ulceration of the overlying mucosa and a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region, which is consistent with a GIST. The patient also has abdominal pain and gastrointestinal bleeding, which are common symptoms of GIST.\n- Diagnosis: Gastric Lipoma\n- Evidence: The patient has a submucosal lesion in the gastric antrum with fat attenuation on CT and ulceration of the overlying mucosa, which are characteristic of a gastric lipoma.\n- Evidence: The patient has a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region, which is consistent with a lipoma. The patient also has abdominal pain and gastrointestinal bleeding, which can be associated with a lipoma.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Statement 1: \"Mesenchymal submucosal tumors (including GIST, leiomyoma, fibroma, and malignant variants) were considered but the lesion’s characteristic fat attenuation on CT favored a lipoma — “abdominal CT scan displayed a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric formation with fat density measuring 6*5 cm in the antropyloric region of the stomach.”\"\n\nPotential Match 1 from Predicted Reasons: \"The patient presents with a submucosal lesion in the gastric antrum with ulceration of the overlying mucosa and a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region, which is consistent with a GIST.\"\nPros: Mentions \"submucosal lesion,\" \"well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region,\" and \"GIST\" (a type of mesenchymal tumor).\nCons: It states consistency with GIST, whereas the ground truth states that fat attenuation *favored a lipoma* over mesenchymal tumors like GIST. The ground truth explicitly contrasts GIST with lipoma based on fat attenuation.\n\nPotential Match 2 from Predicted Reasons: \"The patient has a submucosal lesion in the gastric antrum with fat attenuation on CT and ulceration of the overlying mucosa, which are characteristic of a gastric lipoma.\"\nPros: Mentions \"submucosal lesion,\" \"fat attenuation on CT,\" and \"characteristic of a gastric lipoma.\" This directly aligns with the ground truth's reasoning that fat attenuation favors lipoma.\nCons: Does not explicitly mention the size and location details (\"well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm in the antropyloric region\").\n\nPotential Match 3 from Predicted Reasons: \"The patient has a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region, which is consistent with a lipoma.\"\nPros: Mentions \"well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region\" and \"consistent with a lipoma.\" This directly supports the ground truth's conclusion that fat attenuation favored a lipoma and includes the specific CT findings.\nCons: Does not explicitly mention \"submucosal lesion\" or the initial consideration of other mesenchymal tumors.\n\nSummary for Groundtruth Statement 1: Both Predicted Reason 2 and 3 are strong matches. Predicted Reason 3 is slightly better as it includes the specific measurements and location from the CT scan, which is a direct quote in the ground truth. Predicted Reason 2 also captures the essence of fat attenuation favoring lipoma. The ground truth statement is complex, discussing both the consideration of mesenchymal tumors and the favoring of lipoma due to fat attenuation. Predicted Reason 3 directly addresses the \"fat attenuation favored a lipoma\" part with the specific CT findings.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Statement 2: \"Primary gastric malignancy was considered but endoscopic biopsy revealed no malignant features — “biopsy results indicated the absence of malignant signs.”\"\n\nPotential Match 1 from Predicted Reasons: None of the predicted reasons directly mention \"primary gastric malignancy\" or \"endoscopic biopsy revealed no malignant features\" or \"absence of malignant signs.\" The predicted reasons focus on supporting GIST or lipoma based on imaging and symptoms, not on ruling out other diagnoses via biopsy.\n\nSummary for Groundtruth Statement 2: No direct matches.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Statement 3: \"Although exceptionally rare synchronous gastric carcinomas have been described, there was no evidence of a coincidental carcinoma in this case — “exceptionally rare cases of synchronous gastric carcinomas have been described, thought to be coincidental.”\"\n\nPotential Match 1 from Predicted Reasons: None of the predicted reasons mention \"synchronous gastric carcinomas\" or \"coincidental carcinoma.\" The predicted reasons are focused on the primary diagnosis of GIST or lipoma.\n\nSummary for Groundtruth Statement 3: No direct matches.\n\nFinal Matching Decisions:\n- For Groundtruth Statement 1: \"Mesenchymal submucosal tumors (including GIST, leiomyoma, fibroma, and malignant variants) were considered but the lesion’s characteristic fat attenuation on CT favored a lipoma — “abdominal CT scan displayed a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric formation with fat density measuring 6*5 cm in the antropyloric region of the stomach.”\"\n    - Matches: \"The patient has a submucosal lesion in the gastric antrum with fat attenuation on CT and ulceration of the overlying mucosa, which are characteristic of a gastric lipoma.\" and \"The patient has a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region, which is consistent with a lipoma.\" These two statements from the predicted reasons collectively capture the essence of the ground truth, particularly the role of fat attenuation in favoring a lipoma and the specific CT findings.\n- For Groundtruth Statement 2: No matches.\n- For Groundtruth Statement 3: No matches.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient has a submucosal lesion in the gastric antrum with fat attenuation on CT and ulceration of the overlying mucosa, which are characteristic of a gastric lipoma.\",\n      \"The patient has a well-defined, heterogeneous, pre-pyloric mass measuring 6×5 cm with fat attenuation in the antropyloric region, which is consistent with a lipoma.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n</diagnostic_comparison>",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
  "a5a8492965762723d8be2093874dadafd76500381c3e2e1163ae491e4b00f7ce": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Consideration of invasive squamous cell carcinoma — “histologic examination showed lobules and detached aggregations of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and some prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis…a diagnosis of invasive SCC was taken.”\n2. Consideration of basal cell carcinoma for residual papules — “some papular lesions with the dermoscopic features of BCC.”\n3. Diagnosis of basosquamous carcinoma after immunohistochemical revision — “revision of the first histologic exam highlighted the presence of basaloid cells through BerEP4 and Bcl-2 immunohistochemistry…coexistence of both the squamous and basaloid components.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient has a history of a crusty lesion in the right fronto-orbital region, and an excisional biopsy showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli, which is consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. The patient also had a focal area of uptake in the supraorbital lesion on 18F-FDG PET/CT, which is consistent with this diagnosis.\n2. The patient has an irregularly shaped plaque with multiple superficial crusts, and biopsy results show lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis, which are consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. Additionally, the focal area of uptake in the supraorbital lesion on ^18F-FDG PET/CT supports this diagnosis.\n3. The patient has several discrete papular lesions that remained after treatment with cemiplimab, and dermoscopic evaluation of these papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, which is consistent with this diagnosis.\n4. Dermoscopic evaluation of papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, and the presence of discrete papular lesions is also consistent with basal cell carcinoma.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: \"Consideration of invasive squamous cell carcinoma — “histologic examination showed lobules and detached aggregations of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and some prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis…a diagnosis of invasive SCC was taken.”\"\n\n*   **Potential Match 1:** \"The patient has a history of a crusty lesion in the right fronto-orbital region, and an excisional biopsy showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli, which is consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. The patient also had a focal area of uptake in the supraorbital lesion on 18F-FDG PET/CT, which is consistent with this diagnosis.\"\n    *   **Pros:** This statement directly mentions \"excisional biopsy showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli,\" which is a very close match to \"histologic examination showed lobules and detached aggregations of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and some prominent nucleoli.\" It also explicitly states consistency with \"invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.\"\n    *   **Cons:** The groundtruth mentions \"infiltrating the reticular dermis\" which is not explicitly in this predicted statement, but the core histological findings are present.\n\n*   **Potential Match 2:** \"The patient has an irregularly shaped plaque with multiple superficial crusts, and biopsy results show lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis, which are consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. Additionally, the focal area of uptake in the supraorbital lesion on ^18F-FDG PET/CT supports this diagnosis.\"\n    *   **Pros:** This statement is an even stronger match as it includes \"biopsy results show lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis,\" which is almost verbatim from the groundtruth. It also explicitly states consistency with \"invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.\"\n    *   **Cons:** None significant.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: \"Consideration of basal cell carcinoma for residual papules — “some papular lesions with the dermoscopic features of BCC.”\"\n\n*   **Potential Match 1:** \"The patient has several discrete papular lesions that remained after treatment with cemiplimab, and dermoscopic evaluation of these papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, which is consistent with this diagnosis.\"\n    *   **Pros:** This statement directly mentions \"discrete papular lesions\" and \"dermoscopic evaluation of these papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma,\" which aligns perfectly with \"some papular lesions with the dermoscopic features of BCC.\"\n    *   **Cons:** None.\n\n*   **Potential Match 2:** \"Dermoscopic evaluation of papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, and the presence of discrete papular lesions is also consistent with basal cell carcinoma.\"\n    *   **Pros:** This statement also directly mentions \"Dermoscopic evaluation of papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma\" and \"presence of discrete papular lesions,\" which aligns well with the groundtruth.\n    *   **Cons:** None.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: \"Diagnosis of basosquamous carcinoma after immunohistochemical revision — “revision of the first histologic exam highlighted the presence of basaloid cells through BerEP4 and Bcl-2 immunohistochemistry…coexistence of both the squamous and basaloid components.”\"\n\n*   **Potential Match 1:** None of the predicted statements mention \"basosquamous carcinoma,\" \"immunohistochemistry,\" \"BerEP4,\" \"Bcl-2,\" or the \"coexistence of both squamous and basaloid components.\" The predicted reasons focus on either invasive SCC or BCC, not a combination or a revised diagnosis based on IHC.\n    *   **Pros:** None.\n    *   **Cons:** No direct or indirect match for this specific diagnostic reasoning.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n*   Groundtruth Reason 1 matches Predicted Reasons 1 and 2 because both predicted reasons describe the histological findings consistent with invasive SCC, with Predicted Reason 2 being a more complete match due to the inclusion of \"infiltrating the reticular dermis.\"\n*   Groundtruth Reason 2 matches Predicted Reasons 3 and 4 because both predicted reasons describe the dermoscopic features of papular lesions consistent with BCC.\n*   Groundtruth Reason 3 has no matching predicted reasons as the predicted reasons do not discuss basosquamous carcinoma, immunohistochemistry, or the coexistence of squamous and basaloid components.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of a crusty lesion in the right fronto-orbital region, and an excisional biopsy showed lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli, which is consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. The patient also had a focal area of uptake in the supraorbital lesion on 18F-FDG PET/CT, which is consistent with this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient has an irregularly shaped plaque with multiple superficial crusts, and biopsy results show lobules and detached aggregates of eosinophilic keratinocytes with pleomorphic nuclei and prominent nucleoli infiltrating the reticular dermis, which are consistent with invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. Additionally, the focal area of uptake in the supraorbital lesion on ^18F-FDG PET/CT supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n      \"The patient has several discrete papular lesions that remained after treatment with cemiplimab, and dermoscopic evaluation of these papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, which is consistent with this diagnosis.\",\n      \"Dermoscopic evaluation of papules revealed features typical of basal cell carcinoma, and the presence of discrete papular lesions is also consistent with basal cell carcinoma.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
