    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Facial nerve paralysis considered; excluded because other facial functions were intact — “Facial nerve lesions may cause weakness of facial expressions; however, congenital hypoplasia of depressor anguli oris afflicts lower lip while other functions of facial nerves are preserved.”\n2. Obstetric-related compression or trauma considered; excluded by lack of birth complications — “The child was born full-term with no complications to a mother without medical problems.”\n3. Syndromic cardiac anomalies (e.g., Cayler cardiofacial syndrome) considered; excluded by normal echocardiogram — “Echocardiogram did not show cardiac abnormality.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- The patient's symptoms of facial asymmetry noted since birth, right lower lip deviating inward when smiling or crying, and symmetric facial movement at rest are consistent with Congenital Unilateral Lower Lip Palsy. The normal neurodevelopmental examination also supports this diagnosis.\n- The patient's facial asymmetry noted since birth, right lower lip deviation when smiling or crying, and normal neurodevelopmental examination are consistent with Congenital Unilateral Lower Lip Palsy.\n- The patient's right lower lip deviation when smiling, symmetric facial movement at rest, and normal auditory brainstem response test are consistent with Isolated Unilateral Congenital Lower Lip Paresis.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: \"Facial nerve paralysis considered; excluded because other facial functions were intact — “Facial nerve lesions may cause weakness of facial expressions; however, congenital hypoplasia of depressor anguli oris afflicts lower lip while other functions of facial nerves are preserved.”\"\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms of facial asymmetry noted since birth, right lower lip deviating inward when smiling or crying, and symmetric facial movement at rest are consistent with Congenital Unila