ial movement at rest\" in two of the predicted statements strongly implies that other facial functions are intact, which is the core reason for excluding broader facial nerve paralysis in the ground truth. Therefore, these statements are the closest matches.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: \"Obstetric-related compression or trauma considered; excluded by lack of birth complications — “The child was born full-term with no complications to a mother without medical problems.”\"\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms of facial asymmetry noted since birth, right lower lip deviating inward when smiling or crying, and symmetric facial movement at rest are consistent with Congenital Unilateral Lower Lip Palsy. The normal neurodevelopmental examination also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement focuses on the symptoms and neurodevelopmental exam, not birth history.\n    - Cons: Does not mention birth complications or lack thereof.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient's facial asymmetry noted since birth, right lower lip deviation when smiling or crying, and normal neurodevelopmental examination are consistent with Congenital Unilateral Lower Lip Palsy.\"\n    - Pros: None. Similar to potential match 1, this focuses on symptoms and neurodevelopmental exam.\n    - Cons: Does not mention birth complications or lack thereof.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's right lower lip deviation when smiling, symmetric facial movement at rest, and normal auditory brainstem response test are consistent with Isolated Unilateral Congenital Lower Lip Paresis.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement focuses on symptoms and a specific test.\n    - Cons: Does not mention birth complications or lack thereof.\n\nSummary for Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: There are no direct or indirect matches in the predicted diagnostic reasons for this statement. The predicted reasons do not discuss birth complications or their absence.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: \"Syndromic cardiac anomalies (e.g., Cayler cardiofacial syndrome) considered; excluded by normal echocardiogram — “Echocardiogram did not show cardiac abnormality.”\"\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms of facial asymmetry noted since birth, right lower lip deviating inward when smiling or crying, and symmetric facial movement at rest are consistent with Congenital Unilateral Lower Lip Palsy. The normal neurodevelopmental examination also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement focuses on symptoms and neurodevelopmental exam.\n    - Cons: Does not mention echocardiogram or cardiac anomalies.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient's facial asymmetry noted since birth, right lower lip deviation when smiling or crying, and normal neurodevelopmental examination are consistent with Congenital Unilateral Lower Lip Palsy.\"\n    - Pros: None. Similar to potential match 1, this focuses on symptoms and neurodevelopmental exam.\n    - Cons: Does not mention echocardiogram or cardiac anomalies.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's right lower lip deviation when smiling, symmetric facial movement at rest, and normal auditory brainstem response test are consistent with Isolated Unilateral Congenital Lower Lip Paresis.\"\n    - Pros: None. This statement focuses on symptoms and a specific test.\n    - Cons: Does not mention echocardiogram or cardiac anomalies.\n\nSummary for Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: There are no direct or indirect matches in the predicted diagnostic reasons for this statement. The predicted reasons do not discuss cardiac anomalies or echocardiogram results.\n\nFinal Matching Decisions:\n- For Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: \"Facial nerve paralysis considered; excluded because other facial functions were intact — “Facial nerve lesions may cause weakness of facial expressions; however, congenital hypoplasia of depressor anguli oris afflicts lower lip while other functions of facial nerves are preserved.”\"\