  "bb2d9b03d26be6db3d60ef17469900acf1dbf3ac8977bf88618e070994309db2": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n### Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons (GDR):\n1. Branchial cleft cyst — The ultrasound features of a well-defined cystic mass with septations in the lateral neck led to “The ultrasound examination diagnosed a branchial cleft cyst with an atypical location.”\n2. Lymphoma — Supraclavicular lymphadenopathy was considered, as “Differential diagnosis of the presented supraclavicular mass included ... lymphoma,” but absence of systemic symptoms made this less likely: “He had no history of ... fever, or systemic illness.”\n3. Lymph node metastasis — Cystic metastatic nodes were included in the differential, yet the lesion did not demonstrate “thick irregular walls, or an eccentric solid component” typical of metastatic lymphadenopathy.\n4. Tuberculosis — Tuberculous lymphadenitis was considered (“Differential diagnosis ... included ... tuberculosis”) but neither clinical presentation nor imaging supported granulomatous infection.\n5. Sarcoidosis — Granulomatous disease was in the differential (“Differential diagnosis ... included ... sarcoidosis”) but lack of corroborating systemic or imaging findings argued against this diagnosis.\n6. Cystic lymphangioma — A cystic lymphatic malformation was contemplated (“Differential diagnosis ... included ... cystic lymphangioma”), but ultrasound and subsequent operative findings favoured a neural-encapsulated lesion over lymphangioma.\n7. Schwannoma — Histologic examination revealed pseudocystic areas, Verocay bodies, and “diffused strong expression of the S‐100 protein,” supporting the diagnosis of a schwannoma with cystic degeneration.\n\n### Predicted Diagnostic Reasons (PDR):\n- PDR1: \"The patient presents with a painless swelling in the left supraclavicular region that has gradually enlarged over six years, consistent with a branchial cleft cyst. The ultrasound findings of a heterogeneous round cystic mass with well-defined smooth margins and multiple intralesional septa also support this diagnosis.\"\n- PDR2: \"The patient presents with a painless swelling in the left supraclavicular region, which is consistent with a branchial cleft cyst. The ultrasound and cystogram findings also support this diagnosis, showing a superficial heterogeneous round cystic mass with smooth margins and intralesional septa, located lateral to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and adjacent to the subclavian artery, carotid artery, and external jugular vein.\"\n\n### Matching Analysis:\n\n**GDR1 (Branchial cleft cyst):**\n- Potential matches: PDR1 and PDR2 both describe ultrasound features (cystic mass, smooth margins, septations) and state consistency with branchial cleft cyst.\n- Pros: Both PDR statements directly support the diagnosis of branchial cleft cyst using ultrasound findings (cystic mass with septations, smooth margins) which aligns with GDR1's mention of \"well-defined cystic mass with septations\". The location (supraclavicular/lateral neck) is also consistent.\n- Cons: GDR1 specifically mentions \"atypical location\" and a direct quote from ultrasound diagnosis, which PDR does not replicate exactly. However, the core justification (ultrasound features leading to branchial cleft cyst) is equivalent.\n- Decision: Both PDR1 and PDR2 match GDR1.\n\n**GDR2 (Lymphoma):**\n- Potential matches: None. PDR does not mention lymphoma, supraclavicular lymphadenopathy, differential diagnosis, or absence of systemic symptoms.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**GDR3 (Lymph node metastasis):**\n- Potential matches: None. PDR does not discuss cystic metastatic nodes, thick irregular walls, or eccentric solid component.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**GDR4 (Tuberculosis):**\n- Potential matches: None. PDR does not mention tuberculosis, tuberculous lymphadenitis, or granulomatous infection.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**GDR5 (Sarcoidosis):**\n- Potential matches: None. PDR does not mention sarcoidosis or granulomatous disease.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**GDR6 (Cystic lymphangioma):**\n- Potential matches: None. PDR does not mention cystic lymphangioma or lymphatic malformation.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n**GDR7 (Schwannoma):**\n- Potential matches: None. PDR does not mention schwannoma, histology, Verocay bodies, or S-100 protein.\n- Decision: No match.\n\n### Summary of Matching Decisions:\n- GDR1 matches PDR1 and PDR2.\n- GDR2 through GDR7 have no matches.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient presents with a painless swelling in the left supraclavicular region that has gradually enlarged over six years, consistent with a branchial cleft cyst. The ultrasound findings of a heterogeneous round cystic mass with well-defined smooth margins and multiple intralesional septa also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient presents with a painless swelling in the left supraclavicular region, which is consistent with a branchial cleft cyst. The ultrasound and cystogram findings also support this diagnosis, showing a superficial heterogeneous round cystic mass with smooth margins and intralesional septa, located lateral to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and adjacent to the subclavian artery, carotid artery, and external jugular vein.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
