  "654fe7092717ec2858d968ab675631e0cf38cceed9bbe29778fe663cee8d9e6c": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n1. List all statements from Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons and Predicted Diagnostic Reasons.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Statement 1: \"Urothelial carcinoma was the leading consideration given the imaging findings of bladder wall thickening but was excluded when biopsy revealed a non-epithelial malignancy — “Our differential diagnosis included urothelial malignancy … Biopsies revealed a non-epithelial malignancy with diffuse monomorphic cells.”\"\n- Statement 2: \"Non-urothelial epithelial malignancies (squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, small cell carcinoma) were considered as less common bladder tumors but were excluded by the absence of epithelial histology on biopsy — “followed by less common non-urothelial malignancy (e.g. squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma and small cell carcinoma) … Biopsies revealed a non-epithelial malignancy with diffuse monomorphic cells.”\"\n- Statement 3: \"Non-epithelial tumors such as lymphoma and sarcoma were considered; lymphoma was confirmed by immunophenotyping with CD-20 and CD-10 positivity, excluding sarcoma — “non-epithelial tumors (e.g. lymphoma, sarcoma) … The histopathology was negative for EBV, positive for lymphoma markers CD-10, B-cell lymphoma 6 protein (BCL-6) and multiple myeloma oncogene 1 (MUM1); and positive for CD-20.”\"\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Statement A: \"Diagnosis: Urinary Tract Infection Evidence: The patient presents with fever, flank pain, foul-smelling urine, and positive blood and urine cultures, which are all consistent with a urinary tract infection. The patient's symptoms and laboratory results, including leukocyte esterase, nitrites, and white blood cells in urinalysis, also support this diagnosis.\"\n- Statement B: \"Evidence: The patient presents with symptoms consistent with a urinary tract infection, including foul-smelling urine, leukocytosis, and positive urine culture. The presence of leukocyte esterase, nitrites, and white blood cells in the urinalysis further supports this diagnosis.\"\n- Statement C: \"Diagnosis: Pyelonephritis Evidence: The patient's left-sided flank pain, fever, and leukocytosis are consistent with pyelonephritis. The positive urine culture and CT findings of renal inflammation also support this diagnosis.\"\n- Statement D: \"Evidence: The patient has left-sided flank pain, fever, and leukocytosis, which are consistent with pyelonephritis. The positive urine culture and urinalysis results also support this diagnosis.\"\n\n2. For each statement in Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons, consider potential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n\n- Groundtruth Statement 1: Discusses urothelial carcinoma, bladder wall thickening, biopsy showing non-epithelial malignancy. No predicted statement mentions urothelial carcinoma, bladder wall thickening, biopsy, or non-epithelial malignancy. All predicted statements are about urinary tract infection or pyelonephritis. No match.\n- Groundtruth Statement 2: Discusses non-urothelial epithelial malignancies (squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, small cell carcinoma) excluded by absence of epithelial histology. No predicted statement mentions these malignancies or epithelial histology. No match.\n- Groundtruth Statement 3: Discusses non-epithelial tumors (lymphoma, sarcoma), lymphoma confirmed by CD-20, CD-10 positivity. No predicted statement mentions lymphoma, sarcoma, or immunophenotyping. No match.\n\n3. Summary of final matching decisions: No groundtruth diagnostic reason has any matching statement in the predicted diagnostic reasons. All three groundtruth statements have zero matches.\n\n4. In the JSON output, only include the statements that are in the Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. Since there are no matches, each groundtruth statement will have an empty array.\n\n5. The JSON output should have the statements verbatim from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. Since there are no matches, we do not include any predicted statements, but the arrays are empty.\n\nThus, the JSON output is:\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
  "b9d072e084e8e17e92cf7a9c8b83dc05cb7d55dbb395b82e35905b66804c8983": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Seminoma was the leading clinical consideration — “seminoma was highly likely.”\n2. Germ cell tumor (gonadoblastoma) was considered but germ cell markers were absent — “Immunohistochemical results are often positive for PLAP, OCT3/4, and SALL-4.”\n3. Endometrioid carcinoma with cord-like differentiation was considered but the typical immunoprofile did not match (CK7+, EMA+, inhibin-α–, WT1–) — “The cytokeratin 7 (CK7), epithelial membrane antigen (EMA) positive and inhibin-α, calretinin, WT1 negative immunostaining pattern is more supported an endometrioid carcinoma.”\n4. Mixed Sertoli–Leydig cell tumor was considered but characteristic Leydig cell features were absent — “Reinke crystals in the cytoplasm can be clearly confirmed.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Statement A: \"The patient presents with left testicular bloating and dull pain, an enlarged and firm left testis, a well-circumscribed hypoechoic lesion on ultrasound, and an elevated serum CA19-9 level, all of which are consistent with a testicular tumor.\"\n- Statement B: \"The patient presents with a well-circumscribed hypoechoic lesion in the left testis with internal blood flow, enlarged and firm left testis, and abnormal testicular ultrasound, which are consistent with a testicular tumor.\"\n- Statement C: \"The patient has an enlarged and firm left testis, a well-circumscribed hypoechoic lesion in the left testis with internal blood flow, and an abnormal testicular ultrasound, which are consistent with testicular cancer, although the tumor markers are not typical for this diagnosis.\"\n\nFor each groundtruth statement, consider potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth 1: Mentions seminoma as leading consideration. Predicted statements only discuss general testicular tumor/cancer without specifying histologic type. No mention of seminoma. No match.\n\nGroundtruth 2: Mentions germ cell tumor (gonadoblastoma) and absence of germ cell markers (PLAP, OCT3/4, SALL-4). Predicted statements do not discuss any immunohistochemical markers or germ cell tumors. No match.\n\nGroundtruth 3: Mentions endometrioid carcinoma with specific immunoprofile (CK7+, EMA+, inhibin-α–, WT1–). Predicted statements do not mention endometrioid carcinoma or any immunostaining patterns. No match.\n\nGroundtruth 4: Mentions mixed Sertoli–Leydig cell tumor and absence of Reinke crystals. Predicted statements do not mention Sertoli-Leydig or Reinke crystals. No match.\n\nSummary: No predicted statement provides equivalent justification for any groundtruth diagnostic reason. All matches are empty.\n\nJSON output:\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n<json>\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n</json>",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
