  "9a09b671c1ed2cb7b50ee6e2c9cf4b865b9e295ddf510440c1a62d48e77c642e": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Corneal melanoma was considered because of the presence of pigmented stromal lesions — “Corneal pigmented lesions could also be caused by corneal melanoma or corneal melanin deposition caused by topical epinephrine therapy.”\n2. Corneal melanin deposition from topical epinephrine was considered for the same reason — “Corneal pigmented lesions could also be caused by corneal melanoma or corneal melanin deposition caused by topical epinephrine therapy.”\n3. Prior pine processionary infection was evaluated as a potential source but deemed unlikely — “Despite the patient's history of Thaumetopoea pityocampa infection, this is an unlikely source, due to no association with Exophiala xenobiotica being reported and the absence of infection reported at the time of DSAEK.”\n4. Keratitis by more common Exophiala species was considered given previous reports but excluded when culture identified a rare strain — “Few cases of keratitis caused by Exophiala spp. have been reported: E. dermatitidis keratitis after keratoplasty and after laser in situ keratomileusis, E. jeanselmei keratitis after minor trauma and soft contact lens use, and E. phaeomuriformis keratitis after severe ocular surface problems,” … “Diagnosis was established by a positive direct examination and culture (Figure 3) of the same fungus from the surgically obtained corneal button.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nP1: \"The patient has deep corneal stroma involvement and no conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, or epithelial defect, which are consistent with stromal keratitis.\"\nP2: \"The patient has brown, multilobular lesions confined to the deep corneal stroma, which is consistent with interstitial keratitis with deep stromal infiltrates. The history of an eye infection and the timing of the symptoms after DSAEK also support this diagnosis.\"\nP3: \"The patient has a history of eye infection and presents with brown, multilobular lesions confined to the deep corneal stroma and a central infiltrate involving all corneal layers, which are consistent with fungal keratitis.\"\nP4: \"The patient has brown, multilobular lesions confined to the deep corneal stroma, which could be consistent with feathery or fluffy infiltrates on the cornea. However, there is no clear evidence of fungal elements on corneal scraping or biopsy, or positive fungal culture.\"\n\nNow, for each groundtruth statement, consider potential matches:\n\nG1: Corneal melanoma considered due to pigmented lesions.\n- Potential match: P2 mentions brown lesions but does not mention melanoma or pigmented lesions as a differential. No mention of melanoma. P4 also mentions brown lesions but no melanoma. No match.\n- Pros: None.\n- Cons: Predicted statements focus on stromal keratitis and fungal keratitis, not on melanoma differential.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nG2: Corneal melanin deposition from topical epinephrine considered.\n- Potential match: None of the predicted statements mention epinephrine, melanin deposition, or topical therapy. No match.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nG3: Prior pine processionary infection evaluated but deemed unlikely.\n- Potential match: P2 mentions \"history of an eye infection and the timing of the symptoms after DSAEK\". This could relate to the prior infection, but the groundtruth specifically discusses Thaumetopoea pityocampa and its unlikelihood. Predicted does not evaluate that specific infection or deem it unlikely. No direct equivalence.\n- Pros: Both mention history of infection.\n- Cons: Groundtruth is about a specific infection being considered and ruled out; predicted simply notes history as supporting a diagnosis. Not equivalent.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nG4: Keratitis by common Exophiala species considered but excluded by culture.\n- Potential match: P3 mentions fungal keratitis, but does not specify Exophiala or common species. P4 mentions no clear evidence of fungal elements or positive culture, which contradicts the groundtruth where culture identified a rare strain. So not a match.\n- Pros: Both involve fungal keratitis consideration.\n- Cons: Groundtruth explicitly states culture identified a rare strain; predicted says no positive culture. Opposite. Also no mention of Exophiala.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nSummary: No predicted statement matches any groundtruth statement. All matches are empty.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
