nulation tissue with atypical spindle cells are consistent with Malignant Fibrous Histiocytoma. Additionally, the patient's symptoms of a painful mass and irregular bone destruction further support this diagnosis.\"\nP3: \"The presence of loose granulation tissue with scattered atypical spindle and pleomorphic cells, irregular mandibular bone destruction, and high Ki-67 labeling index are consistent with Sarcoma. The positive staining for vimentin and α-smooth muscle actin also supports this diagnosis.\"\n\nNow evaluating each groundtruth statement for matches:\n\nG1: p63 positivity indicates epithelial differentiation favoring SCSCC. Predicted statements mention p63 (P2) but in the context of MFH, without reference to epithelial differentiation or SCSCC. The diagnostic conclusion is opposite. No match.\n\nG2: Post-irradiation sarcoma considered due to history of chemoradiotherapy. None of the predicted statements mention history of chemoradiotherapy or post-irradiation. No match.\n\nG3: High-grade sarcoma (UPS) suspected due to absence of lineage differentiation. Predicted statements mention sarcoma (P3) but do not specify high-grade or undifferentiated pleomorphic, and do not mention absence of lineage differentiation. No match.\n\nG4: Malignant melanoma ruled out due to negative S-100. None of the predicted statements mention S-100. No match.\n\nG5: Radiation-induced stromal atypia considered. Not mentioned in any predicted statement. No match.\n\nG6: Reactive spindle cell lesions excluded by strong expression of both p53 and Ki-67. Predicted statements mention p53 (P2) and Ki-67 (P3), but not together in one statement. More importantly, the predicted statements do not state that reactive lesions are ruled out; they use these markers as evidence for a specific diagnosis (MFH or sarcoma). The justification is not equivalent. No match.\n\nSummary: No matching statements found for any groundtruth diagnostic reason.\n\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matc